WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.030
- [Narrator] Olympians train
for almost their entire lives,

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:03.863
with the goal

00:00:03.863 --> 00:00:05.774
of winding up here.
(crowd cheering and clapping)

00:00:05.774 --> 00:00:08.370
And that goal comes at a heavy price.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.930
- It costs a lot.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.070
It really, honestly does,

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:15.260
between the training and the traveling,

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.700
coaches' fees, it's quite a bit.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.210
- [Narrator] It might pay off
and they can wind up here.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:21.930
- Because a complete
breakfast with Wheaties

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.440
is good tasting and good for you.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.430
- [Narrator] But to get there

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.550
requires years of financial investment.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.050
- You're gonna be in a club program

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.870
that's gonna cost about 5,000 a year.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:36.330
There's also fees to be paid
for going to competitions.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.110
If you go away to an away
meet, you're gonna travel.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.070
You may fly. You may drive.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.850
You're gonna have to stay at a hotel.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.380
All of those expenses add up.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.910
- [Narrator] Even relatively
accessible sports like swimming

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.240
can cost athletes thousands every year.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.860
Those expenses start as a child

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.900
and only get more expensive over time,

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.200
costing Olympians and their families

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.960
hundreds of thousands of dollars

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.540
in hopes of making it
to the Olympic games.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.920
Here's a look at how much it costs

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.934
to become an Olympic swimmer.

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:04.260
(start gun buzzes)
Unlike other sports,

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.490
there aren't a bunch of
things you need to buy.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.120
The cost of equipment is
subjective to preference,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.410
but competitive swimmers
use special swimsuits

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.220
called tech suits, which can add up.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.860
Men's tech suits like this
one can cost up to $400

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.810
and women's can cost up to $600.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:25.390
- They could last one
meet, three or four swims.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.040
It all depends about how
long you wanna wear them,

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.460
if they rip or tear or wear out.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.520
The suit, it fits very tightly.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.130
If you buy two, three
or four suits a year,

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.380
you're spending 2,000
bucks on bathing suits.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.670
- [Narrator] Swim caps and
goggles are not as expensive

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:43.220
and can cost up to $300 per year.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.090
And there's one more piece of
important equipment, a pool.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.580
Swimming schools like
SwimLabs, a USA Swimming

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.460
officially recognized
professional swim lesson provider,

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.510
has locations all across the US.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.120
Their location in Westchester, New York

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.480
offers private lessons
that cost $349 per month.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.270
Regardless of where young
swimmers learn how to swim,

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:05.430
competitive swimmers will need
access to Olympic-size pools

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.350
for real training in the sport.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:08.250
This usually means

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.610
joining a club's
competitive swimming team.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.880
- [Swim Club Instructor]
Welcome to Meadowbrook.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.640
It's the swim facility where a Phelps

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.940
and fellow Olympic medalist, Katie Hoff,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.630
both got their starts.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:19.530
- USA Swimming's number
one ranked swim club

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.340
in the United States is NCAP

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.140
or The Nation's Capital Swim Club.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.540
In 2021, a club membership
in the Gold 1 category

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.530
for elite swimmers at their
Washington, D.C. location

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.900
will set a swimmer back
about $5,860 per year.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.600
Swimmers start swimming
for their local clubs

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.880
at around 8 years old,

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.913
but usually will start no later than 12.

00:02:38.913 --> 00:02:41.460
- There's a lot of club
programs in the country.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.300
In metropolitan area,

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.640
there's well over a
hundred different programs.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.110
And across the country,

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.930
you're talking about thousands of clubs.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.710
Without club swimming, we
wouldn't have an Olympic Team.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:52.210
- [Narrator] They continue
to compete for clubs

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.140
until they reach the collegiate level,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:55.430
where many of them begin swimming

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.990
for Division 1 universities,
and some continue both.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.100
So, by the time kids
reach the college level,

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.830
they've already spent 16 to
$40,000 on club dues alone.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:09.600
In 2019, USA Swimming had 412,000 members

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.210
in over 3,000 clubs who paid
more than $22 million in fees.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:15.960
They're the governing body of the sport,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.780
responsible for organizing events

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.770
and recording official
times for national rankings.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:24.060
Registered members pay
an annual fee of $60.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.770
That fee allows swimmers to compete

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:27.760
in sanctioned competitions.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.030
And one of those sanctioned competitions

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.320
are the Olympic Trials.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.250
According to Coach Collins,
some elite swimmers

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.970
might seek private coaching
to be more competitive.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.300
And depending on the coach,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.650
private coaching can run a student

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.760
more than $1,000 per month.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.880
Total expenses can vary
widely from person to person.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.520
USA Swimming estimates
expenses for Olympic hopefuls

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.220
can be anywhere from 25 to $40,000

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.170
for proper coaching, equipment,

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.740
training and travel each year.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.410
And depending on the
swimmer's training program,

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.460
they may also need a
budget for other costs

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.560
like a nutritionist, physical therapist,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:00.960
or a gym membership.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.970
The cost of competing in
any non-NCAA competitions

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.320
that collegiate swimmers enter

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:06.700
will come out of their own pocket.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.190
Swimmers who choose the collegiate route

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.880
get access to all the necessary
amenities and facilities

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.990
they need, as well as
physical therapists, trainers,

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:15.500
coaches, and doctors.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.230
Olympians like Michael Andrew

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:18.710
chose to skip the collegiate route

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:21.020
to focus all their efforts
on Olympic training.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:23.330
However, that means the
cost of their coaching,

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:28.140
training, travel, entrance fees
and lodging is all on them.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:33.140
- It is much harder to
be an individual athlete,

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.000
training by yourself in
reaching those top levels.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.130
- [Narrator] Since 1978,

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.380
the United States Olympic
and Paralympic Committee

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.610
has not been funded by the US government.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.250
So, US Olympians rely on
grants and sponsorships

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:49.300
to offset the out-of-pocket
costs for their training.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.010
USA Swimming gives yearly
stipends to top swimmers

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:53.210
on the National Team.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:54.640
They also provide bonuses

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.710
for swimmers who make it
onto the Olympic Team.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.950
USA Swimming spends an average
of $10 million per year

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:02.230
in support of National Team athletes.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:05.840
And according to them, most
Olympic and Paralympic hopefuls

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.810
make less than $20,000 a year.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.650
Coach Collins says there
are sponsored clubs

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.040
who can afford to pay their swimmers

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.330
to be a part of their team.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.450
- 'Cause you need to have money to live,

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.260
you need money to train,

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:18.430
and you don't have that money.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:19.770
Who's gonna help you do it?

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.900
- [Narrator] The cost of
being an elite Olympic swimmer

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.860
is a full-time investment of training,

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.600
both in and out of the pool.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.340
As in most professional sports,

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:30.080
the best athletes earn
the biggest paychecks.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:33.320
And in swimming, those paychecks
come almost exclusively

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.890
in the form of large
individual endorsement deals

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.456
and sponsorships.

00:05:37.456 --> 00:05:41.933
(crowd clapping and cheering)
(gentle music)

